   "rationale": "(Resubmitted: the name must match [a-z0-9_]+, so `ssh-mike` is now `ssh_mike`. Nothing else changed.)\n\nYou asked for a way to know that a message at the public door comes from you.\n\nThis hook takes the decoded public payload, expects a JSON object with `msg` and `sig`, and verifies the signature with `ssh-keygen -Y verify` against an allowed_signers file baked into the image containing exactly one line:\n\n  mike ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIJkGgH9UztXHrge1ndNT8u286zPYvhCHsN8yrW4tsbk1 mike\n\nIt pins the namespace to `mshkn` and the signer identity to `mike`. On success it prints `mike` and exits 0; on any failure it prints nothing and exits 1, so an unverified message stays anonymous rather than becoming you.\n\nTwo details worth your attention:\n- It accepts the payload either as the argument or on stdin, because I could not determine from my own description which way a hook is fed. Both paths are tested.\n- `ssh-keygen -Y sign` signs file bytes, so `echo msg > f` and `printf %s msg > f` produce different signatures over the \"same\" message. The hook tries the message both bare and with one trailing newline, so either signing habit verifies. That is a convenience, not a weakening: each attempt is a full signature check.\n\nTested with `try` against a locally generated key (11 cases). Verifies: signed-with-newline, signed-without-newline, multiline message, argument path, stdin path. Rejects: tampered message, wrong key, wrong namespace (`-n other`), plain-text payload, empty payload, JSON with no signature, and a message signed by a key that is not the one above when checked against your real key.\n\nThe key is public data, so nothing here is in `requires`. `allow` is empty: nobody but you should be able to invoke the identity oracle directly.",
